+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is a scientific observation: a plot reference, observation date, observer names, species cover estimates, and notes. Workers correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, often days after the starting state was fetched, and tablets can be offline for 48 hours. Arrival order is not evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+The record store must choose how independent edits to one visit become a current record. Every policy keeps the original submissions; the choice is what happens when they diverge. The two credible policies are whole-visit revisions and field-aware three-way merge. Last-arrival overwrite is not viable because it loses corrections without showing which submission was displaced.
+
+The decision's consequences extend beyond persistence. It determines the revision metadata tablets retain, what the review queue looks like, and which records can enter the partner CSV. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around the policy selected here.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scientific-intent preservation** — major. The store cannot reliably determine when edits to different fields are scientifically coupled, so a policy that treats fields as independent can combine edits whose meaning depends on each other. This turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*.
+- **Review throughput and export completeness** — major. Review work is performed by two coordinators before a Friday export. A policy that creates review work faster than they can clear it either delays the CSV or shrinks it. This turns on the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* and the goal of making reviewable problems visible before the Friday export.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — major. Rules that must be written, maintained, and changed as the survey schema evolves are recurring operational load for a team without a dedicated systems administrator. This turns on the same operational tenet.
+- **Whole-visit provenance and explanation** — moderate. A coordinator, the science partner, or a later auditor should be able to identify the submitted visit that produced an exported value. This turns on the inspectability tenet.
+- **Downstream design and coordination cost** — moderate. The policy determines tablet local state and the review/export interface. Complexity there is recurring operational cost, and it must stay coherent with the record store's contract. This turns on the operational tenet.
+
+Original submissions are retained and offline capture continues under either option; those are inherited constraints, not differentiating criteria.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Every accepted submission is a complete visit payload plus the base revision ID the worker last saw. The server stores it as an immutable revision. A submission extends the current revision when its base is current and nothing is in dispute; otherwise it is accepted as a competing revision. A visit with competing revisions is unresolved: its current revision remains the latest uncontested one, and the visit is held out of export until a coordinator selects one whole revision or retains the current one. Resolution records the coordinator, time, selected revision, and contributing submission IDs. No fields from different revisions are combined, and no accepted revision is deleted.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name from revision R7 and submits; the submission fast-forwards to R8a. Worker B, offline, adds a missing species from the same R7 and submits; R8b is accepted as competing because its base is R7 rather than R8a. The visit is unresolved with R8a current and R8b competing. A coordinator compares the whole visits and selects R8b, or retains R8a. The other remains in history as not selected.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scientific-intent preservation: **strong** — the store never constructs a visit no worker submitted; every exported state is one whole revision selected by a coordinator.
+- Review throughput and export completeness: **weak** — any divergent submission, including an unrelated observer-name change, creates review work and holds the visit out of the CSV. Queue size scales with the number of concurrent corrections.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — no field-combination rules exist; the machinery is revision lineage, status, and resolution.
+- Whole-visit provenance and explanation: **strong** — the exported visit has one author, one base revision, and one recorded resolution.
+- Downstream design and coordination cost: **partial** — review-export needs a whole-revision comparison view and export holdout, and tablet-sync needs base-revision tracking; the semantics are otherwise simple and shared.
+
+**Summary.** Preserves scientific wholes and is cheap to maintain, at the cost of a larger review queue and less complete exports.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** A submission carries the worker's base snapshot and a structured set of changes. The server compares base, current, and incoming changes. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current record; changes that overlap the same field become review work. Maintained rules define combination for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and every combined value carries field-level provenance identifying its source submission. Original submissions are retained, and visits with unresolved overlaps are held out of export.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A changes the observer list and worker B adds a species entry. The rules treat those as independent and produce one record containing both, with each value attributed to its submission. If both workers change the same species' cover estimate, that visit goes to review. A species identification change and its cover change are different fields, so the rules also combine those automatically.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Scientific-intent preservation: **weak** — disjointness by field name is not scientific independence. Related edits such as a species identification and its cover estimate can be combined without review, producing an exported observation no worker submitted as a whole. Overlapping same-field changes are reviewed, but that does not catch related edits in different fields.
+- Review throughput and export completeness: **strong** — unrelated changes, and related changes that happen to touch different fields, combine without coordinator work, so more visits reach the CSV and the queue stays smaller.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — scalar, observer-list, and species-entry rules must be maintained and extended as the survey schema changes. A wrong rule combines edits silently rather than creating visible review work.
+- Whole-visit provenance and explanation: **partial** — field-level provenance traces each value to a submission, but no whole submitted visit corresponds to the exported record; explaining the record requires trusting the combination rules.
+- Downstream design and coordination cost: **weak** — tablet-sync must retain base snapshots and send structured changes, and review-export must model field-level provenance and overlapping-change review. Merge semantics spread across both sibling nodes.
+
+**Summary.** Reduces review work and increases export completeness, at the cost of maintaining rules that can silently combine scientifically related edits.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions are selected. The option wins on scientific-intent preservation, implementation and maintenance burden, and whole-visit provenance; field-aware merge wins on review throughput and export completeness. The major criteria split, so the decision turns on the team's tolerance for each failure. Extra review work is visible before export and can be cleared, reprioritized, or staffed. A wrongly combined scientific observation is silent, plausible-looking, and expensive to detect after it reaches the partner. The team prefers a larger, visible queue over merge rules that cannot notice scientific relationships. This turns the choice toward the inspectability tenet and toward the maintenance side of the operational tenet, and accepts less export completeness as the price.
+
+Field-aware merge remains credible rather than eliminated. Its review and export advantages are real, and if the coordinator queue routinely prevents the Friday export from completing, the operational tenet cuts the other way. Reopening the decision requires measured evidence that queue volume exceeds coordinator capacity, or a rule set demonstrated to preserve the survey's scientifically related edits. Either would be a new decision; the whole-visit revision history remains usable input to it.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions forecloses automatic combination of disjoint field edits, last-arrival overwrite, field-by-field cherry-picking, and export of unresolved visits. It obligates the downstream designs as follows.
+
+**Tablet-sync.** Retain the current revision ID with each local draft and send complete visit payloads, not patches. Treat an accepted competing revision as queued for coordinator review, not as an applied correction, and show the visit's unresolved state without presenting the latest uncontested revision as final. Re-fetch after a resolution, and do not base a submission on a competing revision because the server rejects it. Keep idempotency keys stable across retries. Tablet-sync needs no base snapshots or field-level merge state.
+
+**Review-export.** Read visit status and the competing-revision list, and hold unresolved visits out of the partner CSV. Present whole revisions for comparison; field-level differences may be shown for readability, but selection is one whole revision or the current one, never a field-by-field construction. Record and expose the resolution's coordinator, time, selected revision, and contributing submission IDs. Use submission history to explain exported values, and admit a visit to export only after its conflict is resolved.
+
+**Both.** Consume the record-store contract and do not introduce separate concurrency semantics. The submission envelope, status, competing-revision representation, and resolution operation are defined in `docs/intent/record-store/record-store-design.md`.
+
