+## Revision and Conflict Semantics
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+At any time a visit has one **current revision**: the latest revision not in dispute, created by the initial submission, a fast-forward submission, or a coordinator's resolution.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Acceptance depends on the base revision:
+
+| Base revision in the submission | Visit state | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| Absent | No revision exists for the visit ID | Accepted as the initial current revision |
+| Absent | Visit already exists | Rejected |
+| The current revision | No competing revisions | Accepted as the new current revision |
+| The current revision | Competing revisions exist | Accepted as a competing revision |
+| Any other known revision of this visit that is not currently competing | Any | Accepted as a competing revision |
+| A currently competing revision | Any | Rejected |
+| A revision of another visit or an unknown revision | Any | Rejected |
+
+"Any other known revision" includes a superseded revision on the current chain and a revision a prior resolution did not select. Accepting those bases keeps a stale worker's edit for review instead of discarding it. A submission based on a currently competing revision is rejected so that the competing set stays a set of alternative complete visits rather than an open-ended revision tree while review is pending.
+
+A submission whose base is anything other than the current revision has diverged from the current revision even if its fields happen to be disjoint: it becomes a competing revision. Field-level non-overlap is not evidence that two whole visits can be combined.
+
+A visit is **unresolved** while at least one competing revision exists. An unresolved visit's current revision remains the last resolved state; it is not a provisional winner and does not advance as competing revisions arrive.
+
+The status of a visit is either `resolved` (no competing revisions) or `unresolved` (one or more competing revisions awaiting a coordinator).
+
+## Current-State Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval returns:
+
+- the visit ID and its status;
+- the current revision's ID, receipt metadata, and complete payload;
+- when unresolved, the ID, author, and receipt time of each competing revision.
+
+When a visit is unresolved, the returned current revision is the latest uncontested revision, not a resolution of the competing submissions. A caller must use the status and the competing-revision list to recognize that the visit is not ready for downstream use. Full competing payloads are available through submission-history retrieval.
+
+## Submission-History Retrieval
+
+Submission-history retrieval returns every accepted revision for the visit, including each revision's base revision ID, author, receipt time, complete payload, and its outcome: current, superseded, competing, or not selected by a resolution. It also returns each resolution outcome that applied to the visit. This history is the source for comparing competing revisions and for explaining an exported value.
+
+## Resolution
+
+Only an unresolved visit can be resolved; a resolution operation that finds the visit already resolved is rejected rather than overwriting the earlier outcome. A coordinator chooses either the current revision, retaining the latest uncontested state, or one competing revision. The chosen revision becomes the visit's current revision and the visit returns to `resolved`.
+
+The resolution outcome records the coordinator's identity, the resolution time, the chosen revision ID, and the contributing submission IDs: the current revision and every competing revision that was under consideration. Non-chosen revisions remain in the history with the `not selected` outcome; resolving never deletes or rewrites a submission.
+
+After resolution, submissions based on the new current revision fast-forward as usual. A submission based on any other known, non-competing revision is accepted as a new competing revision, including a revision the resolution did not select.
+
+## Idempotency
+
+An idempotency key is scoped to the author. A retry with an accepted key and an identical submission returns the original acceptance result, including the revision ID, without creating another revision. Reusing an accepted key with any different submission content is rejected, and the original revision is unchanged.
+
+## Downstream Contract Obligations
+
+Tablet-sync consumes the submission envelope, the meaning of a base revision, and the idempotency behavior. It must track the current revision ID it last fetched and must not treat an unresolved visit's current revision as a resolved visit.
+
+Review-export consumes the visit status, the competing-revision list, and the submission history. It must present competing whole revisions for comparison and must exclude unresolved visits from the partner CSV. Resolution selects one whole revision; neither downstream node may introduce field-level combination or its own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions ([decision](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge; last-arrival-wins | Whole revisions preserve relationships between scientific fields. A field-aware merge would reduce review volume but needs maintained per-field rules and can silently combine edits whose scientific meaning is related, such as a species identification and its cover estimate. The team prefers a larger review queue over that risk. |
+| Conflict detection | Any submission based on a revision other than the current one becomes competing | Conflict only when edited fields overlap; auto-combine disjoint fields | Field overlap is not a reliable proxy for independence, and detecting it would reintroduce merge rules. Revision divergence is a whole-visit test the server can apply without interpreting the science. |
+| Competing-revision shape | Alternatives to one current revision; submissions based on a currently competing revision are rejected | General revision tree with branches; rebasing candidates onto one another | Keeping candidates flat makes review bounded and resolution a single whole-visit choice. A revision tree would defer branch selection into the resolution and complicate what a coordinator is choosing. Previously non-selected revisions may still seed a new candidate after a resolution. |
+| State during review | Current revision stays at the latest uncontested revision; visit is marked unresolved | Provisional last-arrival revision; automatically combined preview | Keeping the latest uncontested revision avoids presenting arrival order or a provisional combination as authority. The status makes the visit's unresolved condition explicit. |
+| Resolution outcome | Coordinator selects one whole revision or retains the current revision | Field-by-field cherry-picking; automatic selection by time or author | Whole-revision selection preserves the submitted visit as a unit and leaves an explainable outcome. Cherry-picking would create a record no worker submitted. |
+| Revision history | Retain every accepted revision and every resolution outcome | Delete superseded or non-selected revisions after resolution | Retention makes export values explainable and preserves original submissions, which the project requires. |
+| Idempotency scope | Author plus key | Globally unique key; key plus visit | Scoping to the author prevents one worker's key from colliding with another's while still deduplicating retries from that worker. |
+| Archived plot references | Accept existing archived plot references on submission | Reject submissions naming archived plots | A worker may submit an offline visit recorded before the plot was archived. The catalog's picker controls new-visit selection; record-store validates existence, not current active status. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions, with divergent submissions routed to coordinator review.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a worker may withdraw their own competing revision before resolution. Every accepted competing revision currently stays until a coordinator resolves the visit.
+2. Retention or archival of revision history after a season. All revisions are retained for now to keep export values explainable.
+3. Which coordinator roles may resolve review work. Resolution records the coordinator's identity; the authorization policy is supplied by the team's identity provider.
+4. Whether an exact duplicate of the current revision submitted under a new idempotency key should be collapsed automatically. It currently becomes a competing revision and creates review work.
